at the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{5t} = \\begin{bmatrix}4e^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}e^{5t} = \\begin{bmatrix}4te^{5t} \\\\ 4e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4e^{5t} & 4te^{5t} \\\\ 0 & 4e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $\\Phi$ can be written in any order." 13194139535826 false] [17592186221132 1145 "Given a linear system $\\mathbf{x}' = A \\mathbf{x},$ where $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $\\Phi(t)$ of the system is an $n \\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our cases, we have a $2 \\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}=[\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}=[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 4{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/bb710709a593f8d185cde601fb5082d7f79ed21af824c28949477d2011479d95.png)\n\n**Note:** The columns of $\\Phi$ can be written in any order." 13194139535826 true] [17592186221133 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/819e3b9672e898801ef7320c1d80246d218ae89e76b3247c86b642a5f5790400.png)\nConsider the system of linear ODEs $\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x}$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix $\\Phi(t)$ for this system." 13194139535826 true] [17592186221133 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of linear ODEs $\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x}$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix $\\Phi(t)$ for this system." 13194139535826 false] [17592186221133 1145 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\mathbf x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t)\n$$\n\ndenoted $\\Phi_0(t)$, is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0'(t) = A\\Phi_0(t), \\quad \\Phi_0(0) = I_n\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the identity matrix.\n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}\n$$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{At} &= α_{1}At + α_{0}I \\\\\n&= α_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t + α_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}α_{0} & -7α_{1}t \\\\ 7α_{1}t & α_{0}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of $At$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 7\\textrm{i}t, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = -7\\textrm{i}t\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr((7\\textrm{i})t) &= e^{(7\\textrm{i})t} \\\\\nα_{1}(7\\textrm{i})t + α_{0} &= \\cos (7t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (7t) \\\\\nα_{0} + 7\\textrm{i}α_{1}t &= \\cos (7t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (7t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n{α_{0} = \\cos (7t); 7α_{1}t = \\sin (7t)⇒{α_{0} = \\cos (7t); α_{1} = \\frac{1}{7t}\\sin (7t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nα_{0} &= \\cos (7t) \\\\\n7α_{1}t &= 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{7t}\\sin (7t) \\cdot t \\\\\n&= \\sin (7t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (7t) & -\\sin (7t) \\\\ \\sin (7t) & \\cos (7t)\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535826 false] [17592186221133 1145 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\mathbf x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system $\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t),$ denoted $\\Phi_0(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n\\Phi_0'(t) = A\\Phi_0(t), \\quad \\Phi_0(0)  = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that $\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & -7{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 7\\textrm{i}t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2 = -7\\textrm{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((7\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(7\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(7\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+7\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{7t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{7t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/4d6fd69672291bf4c39d33a04656258b89a500bc039588a9fd27eb10db328cc3.png)" 13194139535826 true] [17592186221134 1100 "Consider the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$, where $A$ is a constant $2 \\times 2$ matrix. Let $\\Phi(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $\\Phi(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation $\\Phi'(t) = A \\, \\Phi(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $\\big[\\mathbf{a}, \\: \\mathbf{b} \\big]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$.*\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\Phi(t) = \\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_1'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_1(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_2'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = \\big[\\mathbf{x}_1'(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2'(t) \\big]$\n> $\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = \\big[A \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: A \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n> $\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = A \\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n> $\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = A \\Phi(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from the fact that $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L5}$ by $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L7}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L6}$ and substitution from line $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$." 13194139535826 false] [17592186221134 1100 "Consider the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2